 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Yes, it's getting harder to find work in Queensland, but only gradually. Unemployment has been rising for about six months, though it's still low by historical standards and a bit below the national rate.\n\n**Unemployment rate, Queensland (trend)**\n- Sep 2024: 4.0%\n- Sep 2025: 4.1%\n- Mar 2026: 4.1% (steady through early 2026)\n- Sep 2026: **4.5%**, up every month since March\n\nThe month-to-month seasonally adjusted figures jump around a lot for Queensland. For example, they dropped to 3.7% in March and May 2026 and jumped back above 4.2% in between. The two latest readings were the highest in this two-year series: 4.5% in August 2026 and **4.5% in September 2026**.\n\n**Other signs it's harder (seasonally adjusted)**\n- **More people out of work:** about 144,400 unemployed Queenslanders in September 2026, up from 130,600 a year earlier (+13,700) and 122,100 two years earlier.\n- **Jobs aren't keeping up with population growth:** the share of working-age people who have a job fell from 64.3% (Sep 2025) to 63.9% (Sep 2026). That's the lowest in this two-year series except November 2025.\n- **Not because people stopped looking:** the share of people working or looking for work (participation) has stayed at about 67%. So the rise in unemployment is real, not an effect of people leaving the workforce.\n\n**What's still holding up**\n- **Jobs are still being added:** Queensland gained about 50,000 jobs (+1.7%) between September 2025 and September 2026 (trend). Hiring has slowed since about mid-2026, though, and there are more people looking for work.\n- **Better than nationally:** Australia-wide unemployment rose faster, from 4.4% to **4.7%** over the year to September 2026. Queensland is still a bit below that.\n\n**Bottom line:** Jobseekers in Queensland have more competition than a year ago. The unemployment rate has risen about 0.4 points since March 2026 and there are roughly 14,000 more unemployed people than a year ago. That's a steady softening rather than a sharp downturn, and it matches the broader national trend.",
